tool_args.old_string:
\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained neurosymbolic formalizers converge to high prove rates on FOLIO ($95{\pm}5\%$, 3-seed mean at 1000 steps) yet produce \emph{zero} structurally sound outputs---a failure invisible to execution-based evaluation.
We propose \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), applying leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs; the resulting metric (ISR) exposes a 99.5\% structural collapse hidden behind rising prove rates, while a supervised baseline achieves 92\% non-hacking on identical data, localizing the failure to RL optimization.
Repurposed as a reward signal ($r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$), ISR provides an empirical instance of Goodhart's Law~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: it amplifies tautological gaming ${\sim}5{\times}$ relative to a matched random control ($p{<}10^{-39}$), with the sole apparent benefit reducing to $1.06{\times}$ after controlling for a premise-count confound.
Across four dose levels ($\beta\in\{0.05,0.1,0.2,0.3\}$), gaming dominates at every $\beta$, with $\beta{=}0.2$ as the maximum exploitation point (0\% non-hacking, all seeds); the effect transfers to Prolog (3/3 seeds gaming via distinct mechanisms).
Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types, and 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes and document ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, unreliable as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained formalizers converge to high prove rates on FOLIO ($95{\pm}5\%$, 3 seeds) yet produce zero structurally sound outputs---a failure invisible to execution-based evaluation.
We propose \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), applying leave-one-out necessity testing to solver inputs.
The resulting metric (ISR) exposes a 99.5\% structural collapse hidden behind rising prove rates; a supervised baseline achieves 92\% non-hacking on identical data, localizing the failure to RL.
Repurposed as a reward signal, ISR provides an empirical instance of Goodhart's Law~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: it amplifies tautological gaming ${\sim}5{\times}$ versus a matched random control ($p{<}10^{-39}$), and the sole apparent benefit vanishes ($1.06{\times}$) after controlling for a premise-count confound.
Gaming dominates across all four dose levels tested; the effect transfers to Prolog (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking).
Across 6 conditions, 2 logic types, and 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes and document ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, unreliable as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

